/// Chapter 25 of the guide, written from md-docs/about/contributing.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page AboutContributing(path: "/about/contributing", title: "Contributing", description: "Build and test the compiler, find your way around the repository and its pipeline, and change a built-in, the grammar, the runtime or this guide.", layout: DocsShell("Contributing", kind: .explanation, chapter: t("nav.group.about"), blurb: "How the compiler is put together, how to build and test it, and how to change the language, a built-in or this guide.", source: "md-docs/about/contributing.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "before-you-start", label: "Before you start"), Anchor(id: "build-and-test", label: "Build and test"), Anchor(id: "the-repository", label: "The repository"), Anchor(id: "the-pipeline", label: "The pipeline"), Anchor(id: "change-a-built-in-s-props-or-flags", label: "Change a built-in's props or flags"), Anchor(id: "change-the-grammar", label: "Change the grammar"), Anchor(id: "change-the-runtime", label: "Change the runtime"), Anchor(id: "add-a-diagnostic", label: "Add a diagnostic"), Anchor(id: "change-this-guide", label: "Change this guide"), Anchor(id: "release-a-version", label: "Release a version")], prevTo: "/about/static-or-single-page", prevLabel: t("ch.23"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This page is for working on WebFluent itself: the compiler, the runtime, the editor support or this guide. It tells you how to build and test the project, where each part lives, and what to update when you change one.\n\nThe project holds itself to the rule it holds programs to: what can be checked is checked, by a test, on every push. The registry, the error pages, the components reference and every example in this guide are tied to the compiler, so a change that leaves one of them behind fails a test instead of drifting. Most of what follows is where those ties are, so you know which ones a change will pull.\n\nWebFluent is one Rust crate — the compiler and the `wf` binary — with a language server beside it, editor extensions, a small JavaScript runtime and this guide, all in one repository: [github.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent).")

        Heading("Before you start", id: "before-you-start").h2

        Markdown("You need:\n\n- Rust 1.92 or later (the crate uses the 2024 edition).\n- Node.js 20, for the runtime's tests and the browser suites.\n- Chrome or Chromium, for the browser suites.\n\nClone the repository:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "git clone https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent"),
                Line(command: "cd WebFluent")
            ])

        Heading("Build and test", id: "build-and-test").h2

        Markdown("Build the compiler, then run the Rust tests, the runtime's tests and the lints that CI runs:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "cargo build"),
                Line(command: "cargo test --workspace"),
                Line(command: "node --test tests/js/*.test.mjs"),
                Line(command: "cargo fmt --check && cargo clippy --workspace -- -D warnings")
            ])

        Markdown("The browser suites check what only a real browser can — a service worker, WebRTC, layers, keys, gestures and more. They drive the release build, so build it first, then run them from `tests/browser`:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "cargo build --release"),
                Line(command: "cd tests/browser"),
                Line(command: "npm ci"),
                Line(command: "npm test")
            ])

        Markdown("`npm test` runs sixteen suites, starting with `offline.mjs` and `peer.mjs`; each is also a script you can run alone, such as `node offline.mjs`. CI runs all of it on every push, and a few browser suites more.")

        Heading("The repository", id: "the-repository").h2

        Markdown("Each part of the project has its own folder. Under `src/`, the compiler is in layers, bottom first; a module uses only its own layer and the ones above it in this list, which `tests/module_layers.rs` holds it to:")

        CodeBlock(#"src/
  base/                      spans, text helpers, the file systems a build reads and writes
  diagnostics/               every finding's code (codes.rs), fixes and output; the error types
  syntax/                    lexer/, parser/, the .wfx layout/, the printers and the formatter
  registry/                  every built-in's props, cases, flags, events, slots, parts
  project/                   config, themes/ (tokens and the built-ins' CSS), i18n, scripts, data, media
  check/                     sema/ (names, the type checker), linter/, validation, scopes, edits
  web/                       codegen/ (JS, HTML, CSS, the pre-rendered page, SEO), runtime/modules/, motion
  paged/                     the PDF and slides engine
  template/                  the template engine behind `wf render`
  build/                     `wf build` as a library call, its cache, assets, the service worker
  tools/                     migrate, test, verify, audit, studio, agent, init, generate, docs
  cli/                       each `wf` command
crates/wf-lsp/               the language server
editors/                     VS Code, Zed and the Tree-sitter grammars: submodules, each its own repository
md-docs/                     this guide
site/                        the documentation site, written in WebFluent
tests/                       Rust integration tests, tests/js (runtime), tests/browser (Chrome)
spec/                        design notes and plans"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("The editors live in their own repositories — [tree-sitter-webfluent](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/tree-sitter-webfluent), [webfluent-zed](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/webfluent-zed) and [webfluent-vscode](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/webfluent-vscode) — linked here as submodules under `editors/`. Building and testing the compiler needs none of them. To work on one, or to run the checks that hold them to the compiler (`just zed-check`), check them out:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "git submodule update --init")
            ])

        Markdown("A change there is committed and pushed in its own repository; then the submodule's new commit is committed here (`git add editors/zed`).")

        Heading("The pipeline", id: "the-pipeline").h2

        Markdown("A source file passes through these stages, in order:")

        CodeBlock(#".wf / .wfx → lexer → parser → lower → semantic checks → type checker → linters → code generators
                                                                                 ├─ JavaScript + HTML + CSS
                                                                                 └─ PDF / slides"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("Every stage reads the **registry** for what a built-in takes. So a prop added there is known at once to the checker, the linters, the language server, `wf docs` and the [components reference](/reference).")

        Heading("Change a built-in's props or flags", id: "change-a-built-in-s-props-or-flags").h2

        Markdown("1. Edit the built-in's entry in `src/registry/builtins.rs`.\n2. Update its CSS in `src/project/themes/` and its code generation in `src/web/codegen/`.\n3. Rewrite the [components reference](/reference) from the registry, with the command below.")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "cargo xtask site")
            ])

        Heading("Change the grammar", id: "change-the-grammar").h2

        Markdown("A change to the grammar touches every place that reads or writes source:\n\n- the parser, in `src/syntax/parser/v2.rs`;\n- the formatter and the `.wfx` layout;\n- the printer, in `src/syntax/print.rs`;\n- both Tree-sitter grammars, in [tree-sitter-webfluent](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/tree-sitter-webfluent) (checked out here as the `editors/tree-sitter-webfluent` submodule).\n\nThe guide's own code blocks are the grammar's test corpus. `tests/printer_corpus.rs` prints every statement of every project in the repository back in its place and holds it to the same tree, and `tests/reprint_corpus.rs` holds every edit to changing only its own bytes.")

        Heading("Change the runtime", id: "change-the-runtime").h2

        Markdown("Edit or add a module in `src/web/runtime/modules/`, and add a test for it in `tests/js/`. The manifest that says what reaches each module, `src/web/runtime/modules/manifest.json`, is written by a Rust test, so you do not edit it by hand.")

        Heading("Add a diagnostic", id: "add-a-diagnostic").h2

        Markdown("1. Give the finding a code, a severity and a title in `src/diagnostics/codes.rs`.\n2. Write its page, `md-docs/errors/<CODE>.md`: what the reader sees, why, and the fix, with a program in a `wf expect=CODE` block that draws the code. The test suite compiles every example and checks that it draws its code, and a code with no page fails a test. The [Diagnostics](/errors) index lists every page.")

        Heading("Change this guide", id: "change-this-guide").h2

        Markdown("The guide lives in `md-docs/`, one file per chapter, in a folder for its kind of page (`learn/`, `recipes/`, `about/`, `reference/`, `errors/`, `coming-from/`), and is the source of the documentation site: `cargo xtask site` writes the site's chapter pages, the book's chapter list, the components reference and the search index from it, and `cargo xtask site --check` says whether they are current. Each chapter starts with front matter naming its number, its kind, its route, its group in the sidebar, the line under its title and its search description. \"How we write\", §8 of `plans/docs-redesign/README.md`, holds the rules every page follows, and `tests/docs_parse.rs` checks the ones a test can read.\n\nThe test suite holds the guide to the compiler:\n\n- every `wf` code block parses, type-checks and compiles without a warning;\n- every `wf expect=CODE` block draws that code;\n- every link between chapters, and every anchor, resolves;\n- every declaration, keyword, built-in, browser value, built-in function, config key, command and diagnostic code appears in it;\n- the tokens chapter is the baseline, and the configuration chapter's defaults are the compiler's;\n- the site's pages are what the generator writes from the guide.\n\nWords after `wf` in a fence say how a block is checked: `expect=CODE` (it must draw that code), `fixed` (the corrected program after one), `page` and `live` (built, previewed, and editable by the reader), `files=a,b` (one file of a multi-file example), and `no-check` (not compiled, for a block the prose says is not a program). The full list is `Fence` in `tests/common/mod.rs`.")

        Heading("Release a version", id: "release-a-version").h2

        Markdown("[`RELEASING.md`](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/blob/master/RELEASING.md) in the repository lists the steps: one tag builds the binaries, the packages and the editor extensions.")
    }
}
